  "intro": "# Typical tactics and plans\n\nThe earlier chapters get you a good position. This one is about what to do with it. Each line below builds a position you already know from another chapter and then asks for one move: the blow that the whole setup was for.\n\n## The e5 outpost\n\nAlmost every plan in this repertoire runs through e5. Get a knight there with c3, d4 and the d2 knight behind it, then hold it with f4. When Black trades the knight off, take back with a pawn: the square matters more than the piece, and the pawn that lands on e5 usually attacks something on d6 into the bargain.\n\n## The rook lift\n\nOnce the pawn is on f4 the rook on f1 has nowhere to go, so it steps to f3 and swings to h3. It is slow and it is very hard to meet, because Black's pieces are usually on the queenside by then. Bring the cheap pieces first: rook, then queen.\n\n## The h-file\n\nWhen Black plays ...Nh5 and takes on g3, recapture with the h-pawn. You have swapped a bishop for a knight and been paid an open file at the black king. With that file open, castling queenside is often stronger than castling into it.\n\n## Break timing: c4 and e4\n\nThe rule is about Black's d-pawn. If it is on d6, take the centre with c4. If it is on d5, leave the centre closed and play on the kingside instead. Opening the position helps whichever side has the better pieces, and against a fianchetto that is usually not you.\n\n## The Greek gift, Bxh7+\n\nThe pattern is worth knowing and worth checking before you play it. Four things have to be true: your bishop bears on h7, a knight can reach g5 straight after, the queen can get to h5, and Black has no knight on f6 to cover h5 and no piece that can drop back to f8 or h7. If any one of those is missing, the sacrifice is just a piece.\n\nThe last exercise below is about counting rather than sacrificing, and it is the more useful habit. Bxh7+ turns up there, but it is not a sacrifice at all: the h-file has been open since move six, so the rook behind the bishop covers h7 and the king cannot take. Count the defenders and you find a free pawn. Guess, and you either miss it or throw a bishop away in the next game where the rook is not there.\n\nA true Greek gift, where the piece really is given, is not in the course. None of these lines produced one that the engine agreed with, and a sacrifice nobody has checked is not something to teach.\n\n## Model games\n\nGata Kamsky is the reference player for this repertoire. His London games show the same handful of ideas over and over: the knight to e5, the slow rook lift, and the willingness to keep the centre closed for twenty moves. Specific games are deliberately not listed here, because the app has no game database to check them against and a citation nobody can verify is worse than none.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nBringing the queen out first and letting it be chased. Sacrificing on h7 without counting the defenders. Opening the centre while your attack is still being built. Playing the fork instead of the threat of the fork, when the threat was doing all the work.",
